@node Overview
@chapter Overview

tandem has two parts:

@itemize
@item
@strong{The Emacs package}, in @file{lisp/}: the chat, the review of
edits, the colours, and everything else you see.

@item
@strong{The daemon}, @command{tandemd}, written in Go.  It starts your
Claude Code in its headless stream-json mode, one process per chat, and
talks to Emacs over a private Unix socket (a local TCP port with a
secret token on Windows).  It outlives Emacs, so a chat survives an Emacs
restart.
@end itemize

Claude Code does the work: it reads files, runs commands and decides what
to change.  tandem changes how that reaches you.  Claude's edits do not
land on disk directly: they come to Emacs, where you review them hunk by
hunk.  Commands Claude wants to run are put to you as questions in the
chat.  Claude can also ask Emacs for diagnostics, definitions, outlines
and your selection.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

You need Emacs 29.1 or later, and Claude Code 2.1.0 or later, logged in
or set up with an API key, Amazon Bedrock or Google Vertex AI.  To build
the daemon yourself you need Go 1.26 or later.

@menu
* The daemon binary::
* The package::
* First start::
@end menu

@node The daemon binary
@section The daemon binary

Build it from a checkout:

@example
git clone https://github.com/victory/tandem ~/src/tandem
cd ~/src/tandem && make daemon      # daemon/bin/tandemd
@end example

The package finds a daemon built there by itself.  Or download a
prebuilt one for Linux, macOS or Windows from the releases page, check it
against @file{SHA256SUMS}, and put it on your @env{PATH} as
@command{tandemd} (@command{tandemd.exe} on Windows).  If Emacs does not
see your shell's @env{PATH}, set @code{tandem-daemon-command}.

@node The package
@section The package

Load tandem at startup.  It takes about 60 milliseconds and starts no
daemon, and from then on Claude's lines get their colour back in files
you open, and commits get their git notes, before you open any chat.

@lisp
(use-package tandem
  :load-path "~/src/tandem/lisp"
  :demand t
  :bind ("C-c C" . tandem))
@end lisp

Without @code{use-package}:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/src/tandem/lisp")
(require 'tandem)
(keymap-global-set "C-c C" #'tandem)
@end lisp

@code{repeat-mode} is worth turning on as well: after a key such as
@kbd{M-g n}, plain @kbd{n} and @kbd{p} then keep going.

@node First start
@section First start

@findex tandem
In any file of a project, type @kbd{M-x tandem}.  The chat opens in a
tab named ``Claude'', in a window on the right.  Type after the
@samp{❯} prompt and send with @kbd{C-c C-c}.  The first message starts
the daemon and Claude Code.

The chat starts in the project's root: the version-control root by
default, or the nearest folder with a @file{CLAUDE.md}, a
@file{package.json} or another project file.  @xref{Reference}, under
@code{tandem-project-root} and @code{tandem-project-markers}.

@node The chat
@chapter The chat

@menu
