@end menu

@node Sending and stopping
@section Sending and stopping

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Send your message.
@item C-c C-k
Stop Claude's answer.  Your next message goes on with the same
conversation.
@item M-p
@itemx M-n
Step through the messages you sent before.
@item C-c C-r
Send your review of deferred edits now (@pxref{Review styles}).
@end table

The header line shows the model, what Claude is doing, and what the chat
has cost so far.  Each tool Claude uses is one line: @samp{▸} while it
runs, @samp{✓} or @samp{✗} when done.

@vindex tandem-instructions
Every chat tells Claude how to write, from @code{tandem-instructions}:
by default, in simple English with the correct technical terms.

@node Questions from Claude
@section Questions from Claude

When Claude wants to run a command, or do anything else Claude Code asks
permission for, the question appears in the chat.  On it:

@table @kbd
@item y
Allow it once.
@item a
Always allow it.  Claude Code saves the narrowest rule, such as the exact
command, in the project's @file{.claude/settings.local.json}.
@item n
Deny it.
@item d
Deny it and tell Claude why, or what to do instead.
@item RET
Choose from a menu.
@end table

@kbd{C-c C-p} answers the oldest question from anywhere in the chat.

@node Mentions
@section Mentions

In a message, @samp{@@} points Claude at something you have in Emacs:

@table @samp
@item @@src/geo.py
Any file of the project.
@item @@buffer
The buffer you were last in; its text too, if it has unsaved changes.
@item @@region
The region you last had there, with its place and code.
@item @@diagnostics
That buffer's Flymake or Flycheck diagnostics.
@end table

Complete them after @samp{@@} with @code{completion-at-point}, or pick one
with @kbd{C-c @@}.  In the transcript, mentions are buttons that take you
there.

@node Slash commands
@section Slash commands

Type @samp{/} at the start of a message and the slash commands are listed
at once: Claude Code's own (@samp{/compact}, @samp{/context},
@samp{/usage}, @samp{/model}, @dots{}), your commands in
@file{.claude/commands/}, skills and plugins.  They run as Claude Code
runs them.  Four are tandem's own:

@table @samp
@item /clear
Start a new chat; the old one stays in @code{tandem-resume}.  Also
@samp{/reset} and @samp{/new}.
@item /resume
Reopen an earlier chat.
@item /rewind
Go back to before one of your messages (@pxref{Rewinding}).
@item /plan @var{task}
Have Claude plan first (@pxref{Plans}).
@end table

@findex tandem-slash-refresh
After you add a command, @kbd{M-x tandem-slash-refresh} reads them again.

@node Code in the chat
@section Code in the chat

Code in messages is shown as code: @samp{`inline code'} in a code colour
with its backticks hidden, and fenced blocks coloured by their language's
major mode, without running that mode's hooks.  Claude's answers are
formatted as they stream; your message as you type it.

@node Reviewing edits
@chapter Reviewing edits

Claude never writes files directly.  Each edit it proposes appears in
the file's own buffer, split into hunks: the old lines struck through,
the new ones shown in their place.

@menu
* Hunk by hunk::
* Review styles::
* The list of waiting hunks::
@end menu

@node Hunk by hunk
@section Hunk by hunk

On a hunk:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-a
Apply it.
@item C-c C-k
Reject it, with a reason if you like.
@item C-c '
Ask Claude about it; the answer shows as a note above the hunk.
@item n
@itemx p
On the proposed text, go to the next or previous hunk.
@item M-RET
Show the popup next to the hunk, with buttons for the same.
@end table

The popup also opens by itself when you stay in a hunk
(@code{tandem-popup-delay}).  In the chat, the edit's block takes
@kbd{y} (apply all), @kbd{n} (reject all), @kbd{d} (reject with a
reason) and @kbd{RET} (show it, and decide hunk by hunk).

A buffer with no unsaved changes of yours is saved after Claude's edit
is applied (@code{tandem-review-autosave}).

@node Review styles
@section Review styles

@findex tandem-set-review-style
@vindex tandem-review-style
@kbd{M-x tandem-set-review-style} chooses how edits wait for you:

@table @code
@item blocking
Claude waits until you decide each edit.  The default.
@item deferred
Claude goes on; your decisions go with your next message (@kbd{C-c C-r}
sends them at once).
@item auto-accept
Edits are applied at once; you can still read and undo them.
@end table

@node The list of waiting hunks
@section The list of waiting hunks

@findex tandem-review
@kbd{M-x tandem-review} lists every hunk waiting for you, in every
buffer: @kbd{RET} shows one, @kbd{a} applies it, @kbd{r} rejects it,
@kbd{?} asks Claude about it, @kbd{A} and @kbd{R} decide the whole edit.

@node Claude's lines
@chapter Claude's lines

The lines Claude wrote keep a colour until someone else edits them.

@itemize
@item
@findex tandem-blame
@kbd{M-x tandem-blame} says who wrote the line at point: which chat,
which model, when.

@item
They keep their colour after you close the file and restart Emacs: tandem
remembers them in @file{~/.cache/tandem/attribution}, private to you.

@item
@vindex tandem-author-color
@code{tandem-author-color} and @code{tandem-author-tint} choose the
colour.
@end itemize

@menu
* Git notes::
@end menu

@node Git notes
@section Git notes

With git notes, Claude's lines travel with the repository.  When you
commit with Magit, a note on the new commit, in
@code{refs/notes/tandem}, says which of the committed lines are Claude's:
fingerprints of the lines and who wrote them, never the code, and no
paths of your machine.  When you open a file whose colours this machine
does not know, the nearest note on it gives them back.

@findex tandem-notes-write
@findex tandem-notes-share
@kbd{M-x tandem-notes-write} writes the note for a commit made outside
Emacs.  Notes stay local until you share them: @kbd{M-x
tandem-notes-share} has a remote fetch them from then on, kept apart
from yours, and pushes yours after asking.

@node Context
@chapter Context

@menu
