@node Asking about code
@section Asking about code

@findex tandem-ask
@kbd{M-x tandem-ask} asks Claude about the region, or else the function
or line at point.  The question goes to the chat with the file, the lines
and the code, and the whole answer also appears as a note above the code.

@node Telling Claude what to do
@section Telling Claude what to do

@findex tandem-do
@vindex tandem-do-actions
@kbd{M-x tandem-do} tells Claude to change the region, or else the
function or line at point.  Pick an instruction from
@code{tandem-do-actions} (refactor, simplify, document, type hints, fix,
tests, error handling), or type your own: ``rename main to run'',
``use a dataclass''.  Claude gets the file, the lines, the code and the
errors and warnings Emacs reports there, and makes the change with its
edit tool, so it comes to you as hunks in that buffer (@pxref{Reviewing
edits}).  A line above the code says Claude is on it until the turn ends.
You stay where you are; a draft in the chat stays too.  With
@kbd{C-u}, Claude plans first (@pxref{Plans and Org}).

@node What Emacs tells Claude
@section What Emacs tells Claude

Claude can ask Emacs, without asking you, for:

@table @code
@item diagnostics
A file's errors and warnings from Flymake or Flycheck.
@item xref
Where a symbol is defined or used, through Eglot, lsp-mode or the mode's
own backend.
@item imenu
A file's outline.
@item selection
Where you are, and the text you have selected.
@end table

@node Places Claude shows you
@section Places Claude shows you

When Claude shows you a place, such as a definition, those lines are
highlighted until you move out of them.  When a lookup finds several
places, such as every call of a function, @kbd{M-g n} and @kbd{M-g p}
step through them, each highlighted; with @code{repeat-mode}, plain
@kbd{n} and @kbd{p} after the first.

@findex tandem-locations
@kbd{M-x tandem-locations} lists them.

@node Plans and Org
@chapter Plans and Org

@menu
* Plans::
* Org tasks::
* Embark::
@end menu

@node Plans
@section Plans

@findex tandem-plan
@kbd{M-x tandem-plan}, or @samp{/plan @var{task}} in the chat, has Claude
plan without changing anything.  The plan opens in an Org buffer beside
the chat.  Edit it as you like, then:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Let Claude go ahead with your version.
@item C-c C-k
Say no, with a reason Claude reads.
@end table

In the chat, @kbd{y} on the plan only opens it.

@node Org tasks
@section Org tasks

@findex tandem-org-send-subtree
@kbd{M-x tandem-org-send-subtree} on a heading sends the subtree to the
project's chat as a task.  When Claude is done, its answer goes under the
heading, in Org syntax.  With @kbd{C-u}, Claude plans first.

@node Embark
@section Embark

With Embark, @kbd{@@} in its action menu asks Claude about the target: a
file, a symbol (with where you are), or a region.

@node Chats over time
@chapter Chats over time

@menu
* Reopening::
* Rewinding::
* Several chats::
@end menu

@node Reopening
@section Reopening

@findex tandem-resume
@kbd{M-x tandem-resume} lists the project's earlier chats, with their
age, number of messages and cost.  The one you pick opens with its
conversation, and Claude remembers all of it.  After Emacs restarts,
@kbd{M-x tandem} reattaches the chat that was open in the project.

@node Rewinding
@section Rewinding

@findex tandem-rewind
@kbd{M-x tandem-rewind}, or @samp{/rewind}, asks which of your messages
to go back to before.  The files Claude changed in Emacs from then on go
back to how they were, Claude forgets that message and what came after,
and your message is back in the composer.  Changes Claude made with shell
commands are not taken back.

@node Several chats
@section Several chats

@findex tandem-worktree
@findex tandem-chats
Several Claudes can work at once, each in its own git worktree.
@kbd{M-x tandem-worktree} makes a worktree on a new branch and opens its
chat.  @kbd{M-x tandem-chats} lists every chat with its project, branch,
state, what waits for you and its cost: @kbd{RET} opens one, @kbd{k}
closes it, @kbd{w} makes a worktree, @kbd{D} removes one.

@node Commits
@chapter Commits

@findex tandem-magit-stage-claude
@findex tandem-magit-draft-message
@itemize
@item
@kbd{M-x tandem-magit-stage-claude} stages the hunks you accepted from
Claude, and only those; your own changes stay unstaged.

@item
@kbd{M-x tandem-magit-draft-message}, in the commit message buffer, has
Claude write the message from the staged diff and what was said in the
chat, in a copy of the chat that is never saved.

@item
@vindex tandem-magit-trailer
No @samp{Co-Authored-By} trailer is added unless you set
@code{tandem-magit-trailer}.
@end itemize

@node Windows
@chapter Windows

Claude can show you files, in up to @code{tandem-max-windows} windows of
the Claude tab.  It never takes your cursor, and leaves alone a window
you used in the last @code{tandem-focus-guard-seconds} seconds.

@node The daemon
@chapter The daemon

@command{tandemd} listens on
@file{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tandem/daemon.sock}, in a directory that must be
private to you, and logs to @file{daemon.log} there.  It stops by itself
after 10 minutes with no Emacs connected
(@code{tandem-daemon-idle-timeout}).

@findex tandem-stop-daemon
@findex tandem-daemon-status
@kbd{M-x tandem-daemon-status} shows its version, process and clients;
@kbd{M-x tandem-stop-daemon} stops it.  After updating tandem, stop the
old daemon so the next chat starts the new one; when the protocol has
changed, tandem offers to.

@vindex tandem-daemon-transport
On Windows, whose Emacs has no Unix sockets, the daemon listens on a port
of @code{127.0.0.1} only and writes the port and a secret token to
@file{%LOCALAPPDATA%\tandem\daemon.json}; Emacs sends the token.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item ``Cannot find tandemd''
Run @command{make daemon} in the checkout, or set
@code{tandem-daemon-command}.

@item ``speaks protocol @dots{}''
An older daemon is running: answer @kbd{y} to restart it, or @kbd{M-x
tandem-stop-daemon}.

@item ``@dots{} has mode 775, so other users can reach the socket''
Make that directory private (@command{chmod 700}), or set
@code{tandem-socket-path} to one that is.

@item Claude is not logged in
Run @command{claude} in a terminal and log in, or set the variables in
@code{tandem-environment-variables} in Emacs.

@item Something else
@kbd{M-x tandem-events-buffer} shows every message between Emacs and the
daemon; the daemon's log is next to its socket.
@end table

@node Security
@chapter Security

tandem trusts you, your Emacs, your user account and Claude Code.  It
does not trust other users of the machine, the text Claude writes (it may
repeat what a file or a page told it), or the files of a project.

The socket's directory must be private; the Windows port needs a secret
token; credentials go to Claude Code in its environment, never on a
command line or in a log; paths outside the project are refused; every
edit waits for you unless you chose otherwise; and text from Claude is
shown, never run.  @file{SECURITY.md} in the repository has the details
and says how to report a problem.

